# Virtual Memory


```mermaid
graph TD
    A[First Level Cache L1] -->|Read| B[Second Level Cache L2]
    A -->|Write| C[Disk]
    B -->|Flush| C

    A -->|Check Empty| D[Is Page Empty?]
    D -->|Yes| E[Mark as Not Dirty]
    D -->|No| F[Mark as Dirty]

    E -->|Return Empty Value| G[Read Return]
    F -->|Return Page| G
    F -->|Flush to L2| B

    B -->|Flush Dirty| C
    C -->|Update Flags| A
```

# VMM
- vmm_write and vmm_read
- uses pages to store and load (1st level cache)


# 1st level cache
- page dirty - used to mark which page is active and which page is not
- page write dirty - used to mark which page needs flushing to storage
- when page is flushed, it should be checked for zeros
    - if zeros then the page dirty should be reset
    - [ ] add a check for the page, build an 8 bit map of zeros, so instead of resetting the whole page, it should reset just part of it.
    - only sections that were modified should be flushed to disk
- when loading the page, only load from disk the page sections that were modified
- when a page dirty section is not set, all reads from this section should return empty value
- when reading from a not dirty page - just set value/buffer to 0x00

# 2nd level cache
- primitive page caching
- can be set or get
- when pages are removed it executes a function

# logic
- when loading a page
    - check if the page is in the 2nd level cache
        - if it is, return it from there
        - if not, load it from disk by page dirty
- when flushing 1st level
    - it should check for zeros
        - if it is
            - the page dirty should be set to 0
            - 2nd level cache should be flushed - page is no longer valid
            - file section should be filled with zeros
        - if not
            - 
        
            
